## Final Judgement
### 1. Summary of Key Insights
The recorded evidence consistently shows that on October 1, 2020, U.S. President Donald Trump publicly announced via Twitter that both he and First Lady Melania Trump had tested positive for COVID-19. Multiple news sources reported this announcement on the same date, confirming the claim's core events. While the evidence confirms Donald Trump's test sample was collected on October 1, 2020, and that the White House regularly used a rapid Abbott test, there is a conflicting source regarding the date of Melania Trump's test collection (mentioning March 2020), which creates ambiguity but does not negate the well-documented October 1, 2020 announcement.

### 2. Information Still Lacking
The evidence does not resolve the discrepancy in the date of sample collection for Melania Trump's positive test (an October date versus a March date from one source). However, the claim is not about the exact sample date but about the positive test result announcement in October. The direct, contemporaneous reports of the October 1 announcement are strong and sufficient for evaluation.

### 3. Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, the claim that "U.S. President Donald Trump and his wife, First Lady Melania Trump, have tested positive for COVID-19" as of October 1, 2020, is directly supported by multiple credible sources reporting the announcement made by President Trump himself on that date. While minor details like sample collection dates show inconsistency, the core fact of the positive test announcement is clearly corroborated. Therefore, the decision is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The fact-check concludes the claim is supported based on multiple credible sources reporting that on October 1, 2020, [President Trump announced via Twitter](https://www.tampabay.com/news/florida-politics/2020/10/01/top-white-house-official-tests-positive-for-coronavirus/) that he and [First Lady Melania Trump had tested positive for COVID-19](https://www.hellomagazine.com/celebrities/2020100298313/donald-trump-and-wife-melania-test-positive-coronavirus/). While the announcement was widely confirmed, minor discrepancies exist regarding the exact collection date of the first lady's test sample, as one source cited [March 2020](https://www.cnn.com/2020/03/23/politics/melania-trump-first-lady-coronavirus-test-negative/index.html), but this does not contradict the well-documented October announcement.